<p>Section 23(2) adds the reconciliation duty: before proceeding, the court must in the first instance try for reconciliation where the nature of the case permits — a duty Family Courts discharge through counselling and mediation referrals, and one that explains why consent terms reached in mediation find such ready acceptance in decrees.</p>

<h2>Why petitions fail here rather than on evidence</h2>
<p>A petitioner can prove every incident pleaded and still leave court without a decree. The pattern repeats across the reported matrimonial appeals this site tracks: a husband proves separation of many years, but the court finds his own conduct caused the exit — Section 23(1)(a) closes the desertion ground. A wife proves historic cruelty, but years of resumed cohabitation follow it — condonation under Section 23(1)(b), unless later conduct revived the earlier wrong. The bars convert matrimonial trials from a contest about whether events happened into a contest about their legal character, which is why pleadings that merely narrate incidents, without addressing causation, condonation and chronology, are structurally incomplete.</p>

<h2>Drafting to survive Section 23</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Plead the response to each bar, pre-emptively.</strong> A paragraph each on why the petitioner&rsquo;s hands are clean, why no condonation occurred (or what revived the condoned conduct), and what explains any delay. Courts notice the silence more than the respondent does.</p>
  <p><strong>Chronology with causes.</strong> Date the incidents, the separations, the compromises and the complaints — and state who did what to whom first. Section 23(1)(a) disputes are won on sequence.</p>
  <p><strong>Treat compromises honestly.</strong> Panchayat settlements and police compromises will surface in cross-examination as admissions; address them in the petition rather than hoping they stay buried.</p>
  <p><strong>For respondents:</strong> the bars are a second line of defence that costs nothing to raise — even if the ground is proved, the decree can still be refused, and appellate courts apply the bars themselves.</p>
</div>

<h2>The bars in the appellate court</h2>
<p>Because Section 23(1) satisfaction is a jurisdictional condition of the decree, first appellate courts examine it afresh, and decrees granted without adverting to an obvious bar are vulnerable regardless of the strength of the evidence. Conversely, a dismissal grounded in a bar — own wrong, condonation — is difficult to dislodge, because it typically rests on admitted documents and sequence rather than contested oral testimony. Either way, the provision rewards the side that treated it as part of the case rather than boilerplate.</p>
<div class="note">This explainer is general information on the Hindu Marriage Act; corresponding bars operate, with variations, under other personal laws and the Special Marriage Act. It is not advice on any specific matter.</div>
